<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Razor">

  <!--
    Nop.Plugin.DiscountRules.CustomerRoles - task 10.1, the FIRST migrated nopCommerce plugin
    (Requirements 5.5, 1.1-1.5, 2.4, 4.2, 5.1, 5.3, 3.1).

    ===================================================================================
    THIS FILE IS THE TEMPLATE FOR THE OTHER 19 PLUGINS. READ IT BEFORE WRITING ONE.
    ===================================================================================
    Groups 11-15 migrate 17 more plugins. Four decisions below are load-bearing and each
    one fails SILENTLY if it is copied wrongly - no compiler error, no startup error, just
    a view that does not render or a plugin that is discovered and then ignored. They are:

      (1) Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Razor" + AddRazorSupportForMvc=true
      (2) the Content/Link block that puts the compiled Razor identifiers back at
          /Plugins/<ShortName>/Views/...
      (3) Private="false" on every ProjectReference
      (4) OutputPath + AppendTargetFrameworkToOutputPath=false

    ===================================================================================
    (1) WHY Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Razor, AND WHY AddRazorSupportForMvc IS NOT OPTIONAL
    ===================================================================================
    This project contains .cshtml files. On .NET there is no runtime view compilation:
    views are compiled into the assembly by the Razor SDK and reach MVC through an
    ApplicationPart. Two properties make that happen and both were MEASURED, not assumed
    (throwaway probe, deleted afterwards - see runtime-deferrals.md section 83):

      Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Razor"   compiles the .cshtml files. Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Web
                                      would also work (that is what Nop.Admin uses) but it
                                      defaults OutputType to Exe - Nop.Admin needs an
                                      explicit <OutputType>Library</OutputType> to avoid
                                      CS5001 - and it drags in publish/web.config-transform
                                      machinery a plugin has no use for. The Razor SDK is
                                      the precise fit: a Razor class library.

      AddRazorSupportForMvc=true      WITHOUT IT THE VIEWS STILL COMPILE, BUT THE ASSEMBLY
                                      DOES NOT CARRY [ProvideApplicationPartFactory]. The
                                      probe measured exactly this: identifiers present,
                                      ProvideApplicationPartFactoryAttribute ABSENT, and one
                                      RAZORSDK1004 warning that is easy to overlook. With no
                                      factory attribute,
                                      ApplicationPartFactory.GetApplicationPartFactory()
                                      returns the DEFAULT factory, which yields only an
                                      AssemblyPart - so the plugin's controllers are routable
                                      and EVERY VIEW IS UNRESOLVABLE. That is precisely
                                      runtime deferral 1.2's failure mode, and precisely why
                                      task 8.2 used ApplicationPartFactory rather than
                                      `new AssemblyPart(assembly)` in
                                      NopApplicationPartExtensions. Do not drop it.

    StaticWebAssetsEnabled=false: the Razor SDK's static-web-assets pipeline is for wwwroot/
    in a Razor class library. nopCommerce plugins have no wwwroot - Widgets.NivoSlider and
    ExternalAuth.Facebook ship Content/ and Scripts/ folders that are referenced by ordinary
    ~/Plugins/<ShortName>/... URLs and served as plain static files. Leaving the pipeline on
    emits a stray <AssemblyName>.staticwebassets.endpoints.json into the plugin's DEPLOYMENT
    directory (measured), which PluginManager does not read but which is noise in a folder
    whose contents are scanned. Turning it off changes nothing else - the probe confirmed the
    identifiers and the part factory attribute survive.

    ===================================================================================
    (2) THE VIEW-RESOLUTION DECISION - the third instance of deferral 8.1-4's problem
    ===================================================================================
    In 3.90 a plugin's views were LOOSE .cshtml FILES copied into
    Nop.Web\Plugins\<ShortName>\Views\ and compiled at runtime by System.Web's BuildManager,
    so a plugin controller could say

        return View("~/Plugins/DiscountRules.CustomerRoles/Views/Configure.cshtml", model);

    - an explicit path to a physical file. On .NET 10 that string is matched against the
    COMPILED IDENTIFIER, which the Razor source generator derives from the file's path
    relative to THIS project's root: Views\Configure.cshtml would compile as
    /Views/Configure.cshtml, and the 3.90 path would match nothing.

    THE FIX, and it is one ItemGroup: the Content item's Link metadata sets the identifier.
    AssignRazorGenerateTargetPaths (Sdk.Razor.CurrentVersion.targets:718) runs the MSBuild
    AssignTargetPath task with RootFolder=$(MSBuildProjectDirectory), and AssignTargetPath's
    documented contract is that %(Link), when present, IS the target path. So

        <Content Update="Views\**\*.cshtml"
                 Link="Plugins\DiscountRules.CustomerRoles\%(RecursiveDir)%(Filename)%(Extension)" />

    yields /Plugins/DiscountRules.CustomerRoles/Views/Configure.cshtml - byte-identical to
    3.90's path. MEASURED on a probe and then MEASURED AGAIN on the real assembly by
    Nop.Web.SmokeTests.PluginViewRenderTests, which also renders the view over HTTP.

    Note %(RecursiveDir) already contains the leading "Views/". For an Update on items that
    the SDK's own glob created, %(RecursiveDir) is the existing item's metadata, i.e. the path
    from the project root - so the Link value must NOT repeat "Views\". (Measured: writing
    Link="Plugins\X\Views\%(RecursiveDir)..." produces /Plugins/X/Views/Views/....)

    WHY THIS AND NOT THE TWO ALTERNATIVES. Task 8.2 faced the same problem for the admin
    views and chose to RELOCATE them into a real area so ASP.NET Core's own area location
    formats would match (runtime-deferrals.md section 50.1, user-approved). The equivalent
    move here would be to relocate each plugin's views to Views\<ControllerName>\ and switch
    the controllers from an explicit path to a bare view name, which the expander's existing
    "/Views/{1}/{0}.cshtml" format resolves. It was rejected for a reason that is specific to
    plugins and was found by reading, not guessed:

      * _ViewStart LEAKAGE. Nop.Web ships /Views/_ViewStart.cshtml with
        Layout = "~/Views/Shared/_ColumnsOne.cshtml". _ViewStart is resolved AT RUNTIME by
        walking the identifier's ancestors through the SAME compiler dictionary that all
        application parts share - so a plugin view compiled at /Views/<Controller>/X.cshtml
        WOULD PICK UP NOP.WEB'S STOREFRONT _ViewStart. In 3.90 nothing of the sort happened,
        because the views sat at ~/Plugins/... whose ancestors are ~/Plugins/<ShortName>/,
        ~/Plugins/ and ~/ - none of which has a _ViewStart. Most plugin views happen to
        assign Layout themselves and would survive, but Pickup.PickupInStore's
        Views/Configure.cshtml assigns NO Layout at all (task 13.1), so it would silently
        render an admin configuration panel wrapped in the public store's one-column layout.
      * IDENTIFIER COLLISION. /Views/_ViewImports.cshtml and /Views/_ViewStart.cshtml already
        exist in Nop.Web.dll. A plugin needs its own _ViewImports.cshtml (see below); under
        /Views/ it would claim an identifier another assembly already claims.
      * COST. It also means editing all 50 explicit ~/Plugins/... paths across the 20 plugins
        and moving 38 view files. The Link recipe leaves every one of them untouched.

    The third option - the one 8.2 called "option 1" and rejected for the admin views - was
    to prefix the compiled identifiers. That is what this is; the objection there was that it
    depended on "_RazorGenerateRelativePath-style Razor SDK item metadata that is effectively
    an implementation detail", and there was a better alternative available (a real area,
    which ASP.NET Core's own conventions serve). Neither holds here: %(Link) is first-class,
    documented MSBuild Content metadata - the same metadata that decides where a Content file
    lands in the output directory - and there is NO location-format shape that produces
    /Plugins/<ShortName>/Views/, nor can a plugin be moved into an area without changing its
    routes and its Html.Action area preference (runtime-deferrals.md section 77.1).

    CopyToOutputDirectory="Never" on the views. In 3.90 the .cshtml files HAD to be deployed
    because System.Web compiled them at runtime; now they are inside the dll and a deployed
    copy is dead weight. It is also the only thing Link would place at a WRONG path: Link
    drives the output-copy location too, so with the copy left on the files would land at
    Plugins\<ShortName>\Plugins\<ShortName>\Views\ (measured). Deliberate, recorded
    deployment-shape change; Description.txt and logo.jpg are still deployed because they ARE
    read at runtime.

    ===================================================================================
    (3) Private="false" ON EVERY ProjectReference - NOT COSMETIC
    ===================================================================================
    The legacy <ProjectReference> items each carried <Private>False</Private>. The SDK
    spelling is Private="false" (equivalently ExcludeAssets="runtime"), and it means: do not
    copy Nop.Core.dll / Nop.Services.dll / Nop.Web.Framework.dll next to the plugin.

    PluginManager.Initialize does
        descriptionFile.Directory.GetFiles("*.dll", SearchOption.AllDirectories)
            .Where(x => IsPackagePluginFolder(x.Directory))
    and then PerformFileDeploy()s every dll it finds that is not the main plugin file. A local
    Nop.Core.dll would therefore be shadow-copied and loaded, giving the process a SECOND
    Nop.Core - at which point `typeof(IPlugin).IsAssignableFrom(t)` is false for this plugin's
    own type and the plugin is discovered, loaded and then SILENTLY IGNORED. That is the same
    property src/Tests/Nop.Plugin.SmokeProbe was built to pin (task 8.8, deferral 8.2-1), and
    it is why the plugin must resolve nopCommerce types from the host's already-loaded copies.

    ===================================================================================
    (4) OutputPath IS LOAD-BEARING
    ===================================================================================
    ..\..\Presentation\Nop.Web\Plugins\<ShortName>\ is 3.90's, unchanged, for both
    configurations (3.90 used the same path in Debug and Release). It is what makes the plugin
    discoverable at all: PluginManager scans ~/Plugins for Description.txt and
    IsPackagePluginFolder requires the containing folder's PARENT to be named "Plugins".

    AppendTargetFrameworkToOutputPath=false is required with it. Left at its default the SDK
    would append the TFM and deploy to Plugins\<ShortName>\net10.0\, whose parent is
    <ShortName>, not Plugins - so IsPackagePluginFolder returns false and the plugin is
    invisible. Silent, again.

    src/Presentation/Nop.Web/Plugins/* is gitignored, so nothing here is committed.

    ===================================================================================
    DROPPED IN THE CLASSIC -> SDK CONVERSION (Requirements 1.4, 1.5)
    ===================================================================================
      * ToolsVersion / DefaultTargets / xmlns; ProjectGuid
        {D28CAD64-4C88-48CB-8AA4-10B7D050BE56}, ProductVersion, SchemaVersion, FileAlignment,
        AppDesignerFolder, OutputType (Library is the default), TargetFrameworkProfile,
        NuGetPackageImportStamp, TargetFrameworkVersion v4.5.1, Prefer32Bit, ErrorReport,
        WarningLevel, DefineConstants, DebugType/DebugSymbols/Optimize - the SDK supplies
        equivalent Debug/Release defaults.
      * The explicit <Compile Include="..."/> list of 5 files - replaced by implicit globbing.
      * Import of $(MSBuildToolsPath)\Microsoft.CSharp.targets, the commented
        BeforeBuild/AfterBuild stubs, the Microsoft.Bcl.Build.targets import and its
        EnsureNuGetPackageBuildImports guard target (tasks 7.5 / 8.7: net45-era BCL polyfills
        and the MSBuild targets package that enforced them, all subsumed by net10.0).
      * Every <Reference> with a HintPath into ..\..\packages\ - Microsoft.AspNet.Mvc 5.2.3,
        Microsoft.AspNet.Razor 3.2.3, Microsoft.AspNet.WebPages 3.2.3 (System.Web.Mvc /
        .Razor / .WebPages / .WebPages.Deployment / .WebPages.Razor / System.Web.Helpers) ARE
        the System.Web MVC 5 stack: no net10.0 build, no successor package, superseded
        wholesale by the Microsoft.AspNetCore.App FrameworkReference. Microsoft.Bcl.Async
        (Microsoft.Threading.Tasks*), Microsoft.Net.Http (System.Net.Http.Extensions /
        .Primitives) and Microsoft.Web.Infrastructure are net40/net45 polyfills with no
        remaining purpose.
      * BCL <Reference>s (System, System.Core, System.Data, System.Data.DataSetExtensions,
        System.Xml, System.Xml.Linq, Microsoft.CSharp, System.Net, System.Net.Http,
        System.Net.Http.WebRequest) - subsumed by the net10.0 shared framework.
        <Reference Include="System.Web" /> has no net10.0 counterpart at all.
      * app.config - DELETED. A <runtime>/<assemblyBinding> block of .NET Framework binding
        redirects, i.e. an artifact of packages.config. Binding redirects do not exist on
        .NET, and leaving the file would risk the SDK emitting
        Nop.Plugin.DiscountRules.CustomerRoles.dll.config into the plugin's deployment folder.
      * packages.config - DELETED (Requirement 1.3): central package management is in force
        via src/Directory.Packages.props.
      * web.config - DELETED. It is the per-plugin Razor configuration file: a
        <configSections> group naming System.Web.WebPages.Razor, a <system.web><pages>
        element naming MVC 5 Web Forms view types, and a <system.web.webPages.razor><pages>
        element declaring pageBaseType="Nop.Web.Framework.ViewEngines.Razor.WebViewPage" plus
        four System.Web <namespaces>. Nothing on .NET 10 can read any of it. Its ONE piece of
        load-bearing content - the base type and the namespaces - is transcribed to
        Views/_ViewImports.cshtml, exactly as task 7.3 did for Nop.Web's Views/Web.config and
        task 8.4 for Nop.Admin's (Requirement 4.4). NOTE FOR GROUPS 11-15: every plugin with
        views ships an identical web.config, and every one of them needs the corresponding
        Views/_ViewImports.cshtml. Deleting the web.config without adding it produces ~1
        CS0246 per @model plus one CS0103 per T(...) call.

    KEPT: Properties/AssemblyInfo.cs. src/Directory.Build.props sets
    GenerateAssemblyInfo=false solution-wide (task 2.3) so the hand-kept files stay
    authoritative; deleting this one would drop AssemblyVersion to 0.0.0.0 (measured by tasks
    7.5 and 8.1). Notes.txt is kept as plain Content, as in 3.90.

    TargetFramework, Nullable, ImplicitUsings, GenerateAssemblyInfo and the shared assembly
    metadata come from src/Directory.Build.props; net10.0 is restated here so the target
    framework is verifiable from this file (Requirement 1.2).
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <AddRazorSupportForMvc>true</AddRazorSupportForMvc>
    <StaticWebAssetsEnabled>false</StaticWebAssetsEnabled>
    <OutputPath>..\..\Presentation\Nop.Web\Plugins\DiscountRules.CustomerRoles\</OutputPath>
    <AppendTargetFrameworkToOutputPath>false</AppendTargetFrameworkToOutputPath>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    The ASP.NET Core shared framework. The controller binds Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc
    (Controller, ActionResult, HttpPost, JsonResult), the model binds
    Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.Rendering (SelectListItem) and the route provider binds
    Microsoft.AspNetCore.Routing / .Builder (IEndpointRouteBuilder, MapControllerRoute) -
    all inside Microsoft.AspNetCore.App. Same decision as Nop.Core / Nop.Services /
    Nop.Web.Framework: a FrameworkReference, not individual Microsoft.AspNetCore.* packages,
    which would duplicate shared-framework assemblies.

    NOTE: Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Razor with AddRazorSupportForMvc does NOT add this implicitly
    (unlike Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Web, where restating it fails with NETSDK1086). It is required
    here.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <FrameworkReference Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.App" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    No <PackageReference> items: this plugin has no third-party dependency of its own. Autofac,
    FluentValidation, Newtonsoft.Json et al. arrive transitively through the project references
    below, and nothing here is copied to the output anyway (Private="false").
  -->

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Libraries\Nop.Core\Nop.Core.csproj" Private="false" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Libraries\Nop.Services\Nop.Services.csproj" Private="false" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Presentation\Nop.Web.Framework\Nop.Web.Framework.csproj" Private="false" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    ===================================================================================
    (3b) Private="false" IS NOT SUFFICIENT ON ITS OWN. MEASURED.
    ===================================================================================
    With only the metadata above, a build of this plugin deployed **Nop.Data.dll and
    Nop.Data.pdb** into Plugins\DiscountRules.CustomerRoles\ - observed, 132 KB. Private
    metadata applies to the reference it is written on; Nop.Data is not referenced here
    directly, it arrives in the TRANSITIVE ProjectReference closure through Nop.Services and
    Nop.Web.Framework, and the closure item carries the default Private=true.
    ExcludeAssets="runtime" on the three direct references was tried and does NOT suppress it
    (measured: Nop.Data.dll still deployed).

    That single leaked file is the failure mode (3) exists to prevent, arriving by a side door.
    PluginManager.Initialize PerformFileDeploy()s every *.dll it finds in the plugin folder
    other than the main one, guarded only by IsAlreadyLoaded - which compares BARE FILE NAMES
    against AppDomain.CurrentDomain.GetAssemblies(). Initialize() runs from Program.Main via
    UseNopHostingEnvironment, i.e. before anything has touched a Nop.Data type, so at that
    moment Nop.Data is NOT yet loaded, IsAlreadyLoaded returns false, and the plugin's stale
    copy is shadow-copied and loaded into the default context AS the process's Nop.Data.

    This target states the invariant directly - "a plugin deploys its own assembly and nothing
    of nopCommerce's" - so it holds whatever the transitive closure turns out to be, instead of
    requiring the author of the next plugin to notice a fourth project reference they never
    wrote. Asserted at runtime too, by
    Nop.Web.SmokeTests.PluginViewRenderTests.Task_10_x_a_plugin_deploys_no_other_Nop_assembly.

    IT DOES NOT TOUCH THIRD-PARTY ASSETS. Nothing else leaked (verified: no Autofac,
    Newtonsoft.Json, FluentValidation, EF Core or ImageSharp in the deployment folder), and
    exactly one plugin in the solution deliberately deploys a private third-party dll -
    Payments.PayPalDirect's PayPal 1.8.0 SDK, the only <Private>True</Private> in all 20 legacy
    project files (task 12.3). This filter is on the "Nop." prefix precisely so that case keeps
    working.

    NOTE FOR TASK 18.x: this target is identical in all 20 plugins and would be a candidate for
    a src/Plugins/Directory.Build.props. It is repeated per project here because a shared props
    file under src/Plugins/ would shadow src/Directory.Build.props (Directory.Build.props
    resolution stops at the first file found) and would also start applying to the two legacy
    plugin projects that still import Microsoft.Common.props.
  -->
  <Target Name="NopPluginDoNotDeployHostAssemblies" AfterTargets="ResolveReferences">
    <ItemGroup>
      <ReferenceCopyLocalPaths
          Remove="@(ReferenceCopyLocalPaths)"
          Condition="$([System.String]::Copy('%(Filename)').StartsWith('Nop.'))" />
    </ItemGroup>
  </Target>

  <!-- (2) above. The identifiers this produces are asserted by Nop.Web.SmokeTests. -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <Content Update="Views\**\*.cshtml"
             Link="Plugins\DiscountRules.CustomerRoles\%(RecursiveDir)%(Filename)%(Extension)"
             CopyToOutputDirectory="Never" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Runtime-read content, deployed exactly as in 3.90.
      Description.txt  PluginManager.Initialize parses it, and its SupportedVersions list must
                       contain NopVersion.CurrentVersion ("3.90") or the plugin is recorded in
                       IncompatiblePlugins and skipped. Asserted, not assumed, by
                       Nop.Web.SmokeTests.PluginViewRenderTests.
      logo.jpg         shown by the admin plugin list; CopyToOutputDirectory="Always" as in 3.90.
      Notes.txt        3.90 declared it Content with no CopyToOutputDirectory, i.e. not deployed.

    Include, NOT Update - AND the <None Remove> first. Measured: under
    Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Razor, EnableDefaultContentItems is true but the default Content glob
    covers only .cshtml/.razor; .txt and .jpg arrive as <None>. So a bare
    `<Content Update="Description.txt" .../>` updates NOTHING and the plugin deploys WITHOUT
    its Description.txt, at which point PluginManager cannot see it at all. The <None Remove>
    keeps each file in exactly one item type so it is not copied twice.
    GROUPS 11-15: this is the second silent trap in this file.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <None Remove="Description.txt" />
    <None Remove="logo.jpg" />
    <None Remove="Notes.txt" />
    <Content Include="Description.txt" CopyToOutputDirectory="PreserveNewest" />
    <Content Include="logo.jpg" CopyToOutputDirectory="Always" />
    <Content Include="Notes.txt" CopyToOutputDirectory="Never" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
